Installed `SlotText` (`@/components/ui/slot-text`) and its dependency-free engine (`@/lib/slot-text-engine`, `@/lib/slot-text-core`).

```tsx
import { SlotText } from "@/components/ui/slot-text";
import { chromatic } from "@/lib/slot-text-engine";

<button type="button" onClick={copy}>
  <SlotText
    text={copied ? "Copied" : "Copy"}
    options={{
      direction: copied ? "up" : "down",
      skipUnchanged: false,
      color: copied ? chromatic({ from: 190 }) : undefined,
    }}
  />
</button>
```

Change `text` to roll. The component renders its stylesheet once (React 19 hoists it), so there is nothing to import. The letter cells use the global classes `slot-text`, `char-slot`, `char-sizer` and `char-face`, as upstream does; style `.char-face` to color the letters.

Without React, use the engine on any element and add `slotTextCss` to the page once:

```ts
import { slotText, slotTextCss } from "@/lib/slot-text-engine";

const label = slotText(element, "Copy");
label.flash("Copied"); // rolls back to Copy after 1.4 s
```

The vanilla API does not check `prefers-reduced-motion`; check it before calling `set` or `flash`.

Ported from slot-text by Daniel Belyi (MIT); the license notice is at the end of every file.
